      "writtenOn": "2021-02-06T04:33:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think the general advice is to have two methods for stuff that can succeed or fail :\n• onComplete()\n• onError(OemNetworkPreferenceException)\n\nAlso, you\u0027ll need a way to let the client know which of their calls completed. The most common way to do this is to key on the listener – the listener will only ever be called once. Ideally, you\u0027d refuse using the same listener twice, and the simplest (and efficient) way to do this is to simply store a volatile boolean somewhere in the object to say it\u0027s already been used, only client-side (after all, you can\u0027t compromise security with this and the system server doesn\u0027t care if you shoot yourself in the foot). But in this case as usage is so simple, you could also not enforce it and describe in documentation that clients should avoid reusing the listeners so they can\u0027t confuse the callbacks across their calls.\nOther ways to do this involve returning generating nonce tokens for clients to figure out what call this was, but I think that\u0027d be way overkill here.\n\nI think I\u0027d just go with the documentation option with no enforcement. If you want to go the extra mile, implement the scheme with volatile boolean.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 5087,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 5087,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "d5e8efc6efd8c2fc61d5fdbe0d63655e8887e79a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
