        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-06T04:33:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This will crash the system server and therefore soft restart the device. You\u0027re running on the handler thread here.\n\nIf we never expect an exception here, then I think you have two choices :\n• Don\u0027t catch the exception and let the system server crash and the device reboot. This is often the right choice for a completely unexpected exception.\n• Or if you prefer the system to ignore instead of crashing (which sounds reasonable here), remove this and replace loge in l.9364 with log.wtf, so we learn about it. This won\u0027t crash the system server, but will report the issue to us.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9367,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 9367,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "d5e8efc6efd8c2fc61d5fdbe0d63655e8887e79a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b595a762_89f82402",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 41
      },
      "lineNbr": 9367,
      "author": {
        "id": 1470463
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-07T05:53:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m starting to second guess the listener at this point. If I remember correctly, we didn\u0027t have it in the original design but added it after my feedback. I think my main concern at the time was communicating success/failure scenarios to API callers. It seems though however that the standard practice in much of Android is to crash the system server which is notification enough of a failure scenario. I.e., \"Don\u0027t catch the exception...\" as you listed above.\n\nI\u0027m strongly considering removing the listener all together from the API as I am not seeing the same value in it as I did initially. If I do, I\u0027ll go ahead and remove this `catch` all together along with the rest of the listener code which seems to ultimately result in cleaner, more consistent code with the rest of the ConnectivityService APIs.\n\nLet me know what you think as based off of that, it\u0027ll help in knowing with how to proceed with the rest of the open \u0027listener\u0027 based comments. Thanks!",
      "parentUuid": "6140cad4_70c5226d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9367,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 9367,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "d5e8efc6efd8c2fc61d5fdbe0d63655e8887e79a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "264f99aa_a8c86dc4",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 41
      },
      "lineNbr": 9367,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-10T00:33:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think the main value of the listener, rather than provide errors (which of course is also valuable), is to tell the caller when the work is done. This will prove invaluable in tests at least – otherwise you don\u0027t know when it\u0027s okay to test. But also for regular callers, it may be really useful to know when the requested change has been applied as this is an asynchronous API.\n\nResolution : keep it with only onComplete() method without arguments.",
      "parentUuid": "b595a762_89f82402",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9367,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 9367,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "d5e8efc6efd8c2fc61d5fdbe0d63655e8887e79a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f54d779f_e207dc0d",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
